<.shell current={:heading_row}>
  <h1>Heading row</h1>
  <p class="lead">A heading with its action, and a note when the action needs one.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <.heading_row>
      <h2 class="heading">Members</h2>
      <:note>4 sent Oct 26, 18:02 by Alex Chen · 2 replied since</:note>
      <:action><.button>Email 1 member</.button></:action>
    </.heading_row>
  </div>

  <h3>A page's title</h3>
  <div class="demo">
    <.heading_row>
      <h1 class="title">Equipment</h1>
      <:action><.button variant={:success}>Add item</.button></:action>
    </.heading_row>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A page or a section whose main action belongs to its heading, such as Add item over a list.
    </li>
    <li>
      A note beside the action when people need it to decide, such as when reminders last went out and
      how many replied since.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Actions about one record. They go in an action list in its <a href={page_path(:detail_list)}>details</a>.
    </li>
    <li>A heading with nothing beside it. Use the heading alone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The heading is on the left. The note and the action are on the right, the note first.</li>
    <li>
      The note is one short line in hint text, made with <code>Service.Format</code>
      for its values.
    </li>
    <li>On a phone the note and action wrap under the heading.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.heading_row&gt;</code>
    with the heading inside, such as <code>&lt;h2 class="heading"&gt;</code>, and
    <code>&lt;:note&gt;</code>
    and <code>&lt;:action&gt;</code>
    slots. It is the only way to put an action beside a heading: a test fails on
    <code>.heading-row</code>
    written by hand.
  </p>
</.shell>
